<h2>How to Document a Supplement</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>Photograph before it is covered.</strong> Take wide shots for location and close-ups for detail, with a tape measure or reference object in frame.</li>
<li><strong>Notify the insurer in writing</strong> as soon as hidden damage is found, and offer a chance to reinspect.</li>
<li><strong>Write line items, not totals.</strong> A supplement that lists each added item, its quantity, and its unit price is easier to approve than a single lump sum.</li>
<li><strong>Explain why each item belongs.</strong> Tie it to the damage, a code section, or manufacturer instructions.</li>
<li><strong>Attach invoices and receipts</strong> for completed work, permits, inspections, and materials.</li>
<li><strong>Track depreciation.</strong> On replacement cost policies, submit proof of completed repairs to recover depreciation within the policy deadline.</li>
<li><strong>Keep copies</strong> of everything sent, with dates.</li>
</ol>
<p>Our <a href="/claim-documentation-checklist/">claim documentation checklist</a> and the article on <a href="/hail-damage-claim-supplements-what-carriers-miss-in-their-scope/">what carriers miss in hail claim scopes</a> give more examples.</p>

<h2>Why Supplements Get Rejected</h2>
<p>Insurers decline supplements for predictable reasons, and most of them come down to documentation. A supplement may be denied when:</p>
<ul>
<li>The added damage was repaired or covered up before the insurer could see it, and the photos do not show it clearly.</li>
<li>The request lists a lump sum with no line items or quantities.</li>
<li>The supplement does not explain how the new damage relates to the original covered loss.</li>
<li>A code-related item is requested without the code section, permit requirement, or policy coverage that supports it.</li>
<li>The insurer believes the item reflects wear, prior damage, or an upgrade rather than restoration to pre-loss condition.</li>
<li>The request arrives after a policy deadline, such as the time allowed to complete repairs and recover depreciation.</li>
</ul>
<p>Each of these can often be avoided with timely notice and clear support. When a supplement is rejected, ask for the reason in writing. A Corpus Christi Public Adjuster can review the response and help you decide whether additional evidence, a reinspection, or another step makes sense.</p>

<h2>Reopening a Previously Settled Claim</h2>
<p>Many property owners assume that once a check is issued, the claim is over. Often it is not. Depending on the policy and timing, a claim may be reopened when additional damage surfaces later, when repairs reveal hidden issues, when the original pricing or scope was incomplete, or when the payment did not reflect the real cost of repair. Read any release or settlement document carefully, and if you are unsure of its legal effect, consult a Texas attorney.</p>
<p>For TWIA windstorm claims, keep the statutory timeline in mind: a claim must be filed no later than one year after the date of damage, with a possible extension of up to 180 days for good cause, and amount disputes go through appraisal deadlines that start when TWIA issues its acceptance notice. Use the <a href="/claim-deadline-calculator/">claim deadline calculator</a> to estimate those dates.</p>

<h2>How We Help With Supplements</h2>
<p>We review the insurer's estimate line by line, inspect the property (including during tear-off or demolition when timing allows), and prepare a supplement in Xactimate with photos and explanations for each item. We submit it, respond to the insurer's questions, and follow up through reinspection. If the insurer and policyholder agree on coverage but cannot agree on the amount, we can explain how <a href="/services/insurance-appraisal/">insurance appraisal</a> works and whether it may fit.</p>
<p>A supplement is a request, not a guarantee. Some items will be approved, and some may not be, depending on the policy and the evidence. Fees are disclosed in a written contract before any work begins; Texas law caps public adjuster compensation at 10% of the insurance settlement. For a second look at your paid claim, start with an <a href="/services/insurance-estimate-review/">insurance estimate review</a> or request a <a href="/free-claim-review/">Free Claim Review</a>.</p>
